  "notes": "West leads the ♠8 against 6NT. South has only 8 top tricks (3 spades, the ♥A, 3 diamonds, and the ♣A). South can take an extra trick in hearts if East has the ♥K. Several extra club tricks may be available depending on how the defensive clubs lie.\n\nThe North-South club holding should be played differently depending on whether 4 or 5 tricks are required. After winning West's spade opening lead, South should take the heart finesse before playing clubs in an effort to find out how many club tricks are needed.\n\nWhen the heart finesse wins, South can afford to safety play clubs for 4 tricks. South should start by cashing the ♣A. When the ♣K drops an overtrick can actually be made by finessing against East's ♣J.\n\nIf both opponents follow with small clubs, South should cross to the dummy and lead a club towards the closed hand. By playing clubs in this manner, the contract will fail only when West has 3 or more clubs including both the ♣K and ♣J (in which case there was never any hope).\n\nIf West has the ♥K, South will need 5 club tricks. South can no longer afford to play the ♣A. South wins the return and should try a club to the ♣Q. West must have either a singleton ♣J or a doubleton club not including the ♣K.\n\nThe concept of playing hearts before clubs is an example of what is called a discovery play. By playing hearts first, South can discover his objective in the club suit.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♠8 against 6NT. South has only 8 top tricks (3 spades, the ♥A, 3 diamonds, and the ♣A). South can take an extra trick in hearts if East has the ♥K. Several extra club tricks may be available depending on how the defensive clubs lie.",
    "The North-South club holding should be played differently depending on whether 4 or 5 tricks are required. After winning West's spade opening lead, South should take the heart finesse before playing clubs in an effort to find out how many club tricks are needed.",
    "When the heart finesse wins, South can afford to safety play clubs for 4 tricks. South should start by cashing the ♣A. When the ♣K drops an overtrick can actually be made by finessing against East's ♣J.",
    "If both opponents follow with small clubs, South should cross to the dummy and lead a club towards the closed hand. By playing clubs in this manner, the contract will fail only when West has 3 or more clubs including both the ♣K and ♣J (in which case there was never any hope).",
    "If West has the ♥K, South will need 5 club tricks. South can no longer afford to play the ♣A. South wins the return and should try a club to the ♣Q. West must have either a singleton ♣J or a doubleton club not including the ♣K.",
    "The concept of playing hearts before clubs is an example of what is called a discovery play. By playing hearts first, South can discover his objective in the club suit."
